2.37.m:
He is the eternal [lord] of the gods, the sages, and the ancestors;
at the end of a thousand cosmic ages, at the dissolution of all embodied beings,
this blessed Maheśvara destroys, having become Time.

2.43.m:
Some resemble mountains, some are like herds of elephants;
others resemble heaps of burning coals, and some carry shoals of fish;
multiform, terrible in appearance, making frightful roaring sounds.

Then all the rain-bearing clouds fill the vault of the sky;
then those roaring, terrible clouds, born from the sun,
enfolded sevenfold within themselves, extinguish that fire.

Thus the earth with its mountains and continents is gradually covered by waters;
the water drunk by the sun's rays stays in the clouds,
falls again to the earth, and by it the oceans are filled.

2.44.m:
and the greatness of Vārāṇasī, and the description of the sacred places,
and the pilgrimage to sacred places by Vyāsa, and then the vision of the Goddess,
and the banishment is told, and likewise the granting of a boon;
